title: Debugging and profiling
card: Chapter 15 | Debugging | Stopping a program to look inside
say:
  When a program does something you didn't expect, it helps to stop it partway, and look at what its names hold.
uncard

new: average.fathom
type fast:
  to the average of, given scores (a list of numbers), returning a number:
    set total to 0
    for each s in scores:
      set total to total plus s
    return total divided by the size of scores
  and the universe said i love you

  genesis:
    set marks to a list containing (7, 9, 4, 10)
    set middle to the average of marks
    scrawl "The average is ", middle
  and the universe said i love you
stop at: 4
zoom: lines 1-6
spot: line 4
say:
  Clicking beside a line's number marks it with a red dot: a stop. When the program is debugged, it stops each time it reaches that line.
spot: off
zoom: out
debug
zoom: #output
say:
  Debug runs it. It stopped at line four, before running it, and shows every name the command has: the scores, the total so far, and s.
wait
zoom: out
click: #go
until paused
zoom: #output
say: Go carries on to the next stop. It's line four again, the next time round the loop, and the total has grown.
wait
zoom: out
stop at: 4
click: #step
until paused
zoom: #output
say:
  That click took the stop away. Step runs one line, and stops at the next. Over runs a line and every command it calls, and Out runs to the end of this command.
wait
zoom: out
click: #go
until finished
zoom: #output
say: With no stops left, Go runs to the end.
wait
zoom: out

card: Chapter 15 | Profiling | Finding what is slow
say:
  When a program is slow, the profiler shows where the time goes: which lines ran most, and how long each took.
uncard
new: primes.fathom
type fast:
  to is prime, given n (a number), returning yes or no:
    if n falls below 2: return no
    for each d from 2 to n minus 1:
      if remainder of (n, d) is 0: return no
    return yes
  and the universe said i love you

  genesis:
    set found to 0
    for each n from 1 to 3000:
      if is prime with n: set found to found plus 1
    scrawl found, " primes below 3000"
  and the universe said i love you
profile
zoom: #output
say:
  Profile runs it, then lists the slowest lines. Line four ran six hundred thousand times. It and the loop around it, line three, take nearly all the time.
  The loop tries every number below n. But a number that isn't prime always has a divisor no bigger than its square root.
wait
zoom: out
line: 3
keys: backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace backspace
type: square root of n:
profile
zoom: #output
say: Stopping at the square root, it runs far fewer times, and the whole program is many times faster.
wait
zoom: out
